    "title": "Homework 2 - Member A",
    "section": "Task 2: Basic Data Frame Practice",
    "text": "Task 2: Basic Data Frame Practice\n\n1) Create a data frame with four columns\n\n# create a separate character vector for the subject names\npatient &lt;- names(pre_bp)\n\n#create data frame using `data.frame()` function\nbp_df &lt;- data.frame(patient, pre_bp, post_bp, diff_bp)\nbp_df\n\n              patient pre_bp post_bp diff_bp\nSubject_1   Subject_1    130     114      16\nSubject_2   Subject_2    128      98      30\nSubject_3   Subject_3    116     113       3\nSubject_4   Subject_4    124      99      25\nSubject_5   Subject_5    133     107      26\nSubject_6   Subject_6    134     116      18\nSubject_7   Subject_7    118     113       5\nSubject_8   Subject_8    126     111      15\nSubject_9   Subject_9    114     119      -5\nSubject_10 Subject_10    127     117      10\nSubject_11 Subject_11    141     101      40\nSubject_12 Subject_12    138     119      19\nSubject_13 Subject_13    128     130      -2\nSubject_14 Subject_14    140     122      18\nSubject_15 Subject_15    137     106      31\nSubject_16 Subject_16    131     106      25\nSubject_17 Subject_17    120     124      -4\nSubject_18 Subject_18    128     102      26\nSubject_19 Subject_19    139     117      22\nSubject_20 Subject_20    135     113      22\n\n\nThe big teaching here is that the subject names, up until this step, were stored as the names attribute of the vectors and not as a separate column. In order to use them as the patient column in the data frame we first extracted them into their own character vector. We also note that vector names can automatically become data frame row names, but that row names are not the same thing as a data frame column.\n\n\n2) Return data frame rows where diff_bp column is negative\n\n#from the data frame, keep rows where `diff_bp` is negative\nbp_df[bp_df$diff_bp &lt; 0, ]\n\n              patient pre_bp post_bp diff_bp\nSubject_9   Subject_9    114     119      -5\nSubject_13 Subject_13    128     130      -2\nSubject_17 Subject_17    120     124      -4\n\n\nThis confirms that of the 20 subjects, three of them saw negative differences between the pre and post treatments.\n\n\n3) Add a new column using a condition\n\n# identify in data frame where post_bp is less than 120 \nbp_df$post_bp &lt; 120 # this creates a logical vector\n\n [1]  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE\n[13] FALSE FALSE  TRUE  TRUE FALSE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE\n\n#create a new column with the logical vector\nbp_df$less_120 &lt;- bp_df$post_bp &lt; 120\n\nWorth noting that the comparison bp_df$post_bp &lt; 120produced a logical vector, but it did not need to be stored as a separate vector object in order to assign it directly to the data frame column. Alternatively, we could have saved the logical vector as a separate vector object and then assigned it to the data frame, but this would have introduced an extra step.\n\n\n4) Print the data frame in document\n\n#print new data frame in document\nknitr::kable(bp_df)\n\n\n\n\n\npatient\npre_bp\npost_bp\ndiff_bp\nless_120\n\n\n\n\nSubject_1\nSubject_1\n130\n114\n16\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_2\nSubject_2\n128\n98\n30\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_3\nSubject_3\n116\n113\n3\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_4\nSubject_4\n124\n99\n25\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_5\nSubject_5\n133\n107\n26\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_6\nSubject_6\n134\n116\n18\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_7\nSubject_7\n118\n113\n5\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_8\nSubject_8\n126\n111\n15\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_9\nSubject_9\n114\n119\n-5\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_10\nSubject_10\n127\n117\n10\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_11\nSubject_11\n141\n101\n40\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_12\nSubject_12\n138\n119\n19\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_13\nSubject_13\n128\n130\n-2\nFALSE\n\n\nSubject_14\nSubject_14\n140\n122\n18\nFALSE\n\n\nSubject_15\nSubject_15\n137\n106\n31\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_16\nSubject_16\n131\n106\n25\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_17\nSubject_17\n120\n124\n-4\nFALSE\n\n\nSubject_18\nSubject_18\n128\n102\n26\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_19\nSubject_19\n139\n117\n22\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_20\nSubject_20\n135\n113\n22\nTRUE"
