    "title": "HW2_B",
    "section": "",
    "text": "Task 1: Basic Vector Practice\n\n1) Create two vectors using the c() function\n\n# create vector with pre measurements using combine function\npre_bp &lt;- c(130, 128, 116, 124, 133, 134, 118, 126, 114, 127, 141, 138, 128, 140, 137, 131, 120, 128, 139, 135)\n\n# create vector with post measurements using combine function\npost_bp &lt;- c(114, 98, 113, 99, 107, 116, 113, 111, 119, 117, 101, 119, 130, 122, 106, 106, 124, 102, 117, 113)\n\n\n\n2) Assign names to the vector elements using the paste() function\n\n# create names for the 20 subjects using a character vector\nnames(pre_bp) &lt;- paste(\"Subject\", 1:20, sep = \"_\")\nnames(post_bp) &lt;- paste(\"Subject\", 1:20, sep = \"_\")\n\npre_bp\n\n Subject_1  Subject_2  Subject_3  Subject_4  Subject_5  Subject_6  Subject_7 \n       130        128        116        124        133        134        118 \n Subject_8  Subject_9 Subject_10 Subject_11 Subject_12 Subject_13 Subject_14 \n       126        114        127        141        138        128        140 \nSubject_15 Subject_16 Subject_17 Subject_18 Subject_19 Subject_20 \n       137        131        120        128        139        135 \n\npost_bp\n\n Subject_1  Subject_2  Subject_3  Subject_4  Subject_5  Subject_6  Subject_7 \n       114         98        113         99        107        116        113 \n Subject_8  Subject_9 Subject_10 Subject_11 Subject_12 Subject_13 Subject_14 \n       111        119        117        101        119        130        122 \nSubject_15 Subject_16 Subject_17 Subject_18 Subject_19 Subject_20 \n       106        106        124        102        117        113 \n\n\n\n\n3) Calculate change in blood pressure\n\n#subtract the post treatment level from pre treatment level for each subject and store as vector\ndiff_bp &lt;- (pre_bp - post_bp)\ndiff_bp\n\n Subject_1  Subject_2  Subject_3  Subject_4  Subject_5  Subject_6  Subject_7 \n        16         30          3         25         26         18          5 \n Subject_8  Subject_9 Subject_10 Subject_11 Subject_12 Subject_13 Subject_14 \n        15         -5         10         40         19         -2         18 \nSubject_15 Subject_16 Subject_17 Subject_18 Subject_19 Subject_20 \n        31         25         -4         26         22         22 \n\n\nThis shows that two numeric vectors of equal length can be subtracted directly, and R performs the operation element by element. Of the 20 subjects, all but three showed decreases in blood pressure.\n\n\n4) Calculate average change in blood pressure\n\nmean(diff_bp)\n\n[1] 17\n\n\nThe average decrease in blood pressure was 17.\n\n\n5) Identify which subjects showed a decrease in blood pressure\n\n# we use a logical operator to determine positive values\nwhich(diff_bp &gt; 0)\n\n Subject_1  Subject_2  Subject_3  Subject_4  Subject_5  Subject_6  Subject_7 \n         1          2          3          4          5          6          7 \n Subject_8 Subject_10 Subject_11 Subject_12 Subject_14 Subject_15 Subject_16 \n         8         10         11         12         14         15         16 \nSubject_18 Subject_19 Subject_20 \n        18         19         20 \n\n\nThe output gives the indices and names associated with positive changes.\n\n\n6) Subset the vector of differences for only positive differences\n\n#one way of doing this is to create a position vector of positive differences\npos &lt;- which(diff_bp &gt; 0)\n\n# returns the elements of the positions listed in pos\ndiff_bp[pos]\n\n Subject_1  Subject_2  Subject_3  Subject_4  Subject_5  Subject_6  Subject_7 \n        16         30          3         25         26         18          5 \n Subject_8 Subject_10 Subject_11 Subject_12 Subject_14 Subject_15 Subject_16 \n        15         10         40         19         18         31         25 \nSubject_18 Subject_19 Subject_20 \n        26         22         22 \n\n\nWe use [] in R to subset or select certain elements from an object.\n\n\n7) Calculate average change in blood pressure for positive differences\n\nmean(diff_bp[pos])\n\n[1] 20.64706\n\n\nThe average decrease in blood pressure was 20.65.\n\n\n\nTask 2: Basic Data Frame Practice\n\n1) Create a data frame with four columns\n\n# create a separate character vector for the subject names\npatient &lt;- names(pre_bp)\n\n#create data frame using `data.frame()` function\nbp_df1 &lt;- data.frame(patient, pre_bp, post_bp, diff_bp)\nbp_df1\n\n              patient pre_bp post_bp diff_bp\nSubject_1   Subject_1    130     114      16\nSubject_2   Subject_2    128      98      30\nSubject_3   Subject_3    116     113       3\nSubject_4   Subject_4    124      99      25\nSubject_5   Subject_5    133     107      26\nSubject_6   Subject_6    134     116      18\nSubject_7   Subject_7    118     113       5\nSubject_8   Subject_8    126     111      15\nSubject_9   Subject_9    114     119      -5\nSubject_10 Subject_10    127     117      10\nSubject_11 Subject_11    141     101      40\nSubject_12 Subject_12    138     119      19\nSubject_13 Subject_13    128     130      -2\nSubject_14 Subject_14    140     122      18\nSubject_15 Subject_15    137     106      31\nSubject_16 Subject_16    131     106      25\nSubject_17 Subject_17    120     124      -4\nSubject_18 Subject_18    128     102      26\nSubject_19 Subject_19    139     117      22\nSubject_20 Subject_20    135     113      22\n\n\nThe big teaching here is that the subject names, up until this step, were stored as the names attribute of the vectors and not as a separate column. In order to use them as the patient column in the data frame we first extracted them into their own character vector. We also note that vector names can automatically become data frame row names, but that row names are not the same thing as a data frame column.\n\n\n2) Return data frame rows where diff column is negative\n\n#from the data frame, keep rows where `diff` is negative\nbp_df1[bp_df1$diff &lt; 0, ]\n\n              patient pre_bp post_bp diff_bp\nSubject_9   Subject_9    114     119      -5\nSubject_13 Subject_13    128     130      -2\nSubject_17 Subject_17    120     124      -4\n\n\nThis confirms that of the 20 subjects, three of them saw negative differences between the pre and post treatments.\n\n\n3) Add a new column using a condition\n\n# identify in data frame where post is less than 120 \nbp_df1$post &lt; 120 # this creates a logical vector\n\n [1]  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE\n[13] FALSE FALSE  TRUE  TRUE FALSE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE\n\n#create a new column with the logical vector\nbp_df1$less_120 &lt;- bp_df1$post &lt; 120\n\nThe key teaching here is that the comparison bp_df$post_bp &lt; 120produced a logical vector, but it did not need to be stored as a separate vector object in order to assign it directly to the data frame column. Alternatively, we could have saved the logical vector as a separate vector object and then assigned it to the data frame, but this would have introduced an extra step.\n\n\n4) Print the data frame out nicely\n\nknitr::kable(bp_df1)\n\nWarning in attr(x, \"align\"): 'xfun::attr()' is deprecated.\nUse 'xfun::attr2()' instead.\nSee help(\"Deprecated\")\n\n\nWarning in attr(x, \"format\"): 'xfun::attr()' is deprecated.\nUse 'xfun::attr2()' instead.\nSee help(\"Deprecated\")\n\n\n\n\n\n\npatient\npre_bp\npost_bp\ndiff_bp\nless_120\n\n\n\n\nSubject_1\nSubject_1\n130\n114\n16\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_2\nSubject_2\n128\n98\n30\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_3\nSubject_3\n116\n113\n3\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_4\nSubject_4\n124\n99\n25\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_5\nSubject_5\n133\n107\n26\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_6\nSubject_6\n134\n116\n18\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_7\nSubject_7\n118\n113\n5\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_8\nSubject_8\n126\n111\n15\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_9\nSubject_9\n114\n119\n-5\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_10\nSubject_10\n127\n117\n10\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_11\nSubject_11\n141\n101\n40\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_12\nSubject_12\n138\n119\n19\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_13\nSubject_13\n128\n130\n-2\nFALSE\n\n\nSubject_14\nSubject_14\n140\n122\n18\nFALSE\n\n\nSubject_15\nSubject_15\n137\n106\n31\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_16\nSubject_16\n131\n106\n25\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_17\nSubject_17\n120\n124\n-4\nFALSE\n\n\nSubject_18\nSubject_18\n128\n102\n26\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_19\nSubject_19\n139\n117\n22\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_20\nSubject_20\n135\n113\n22\nTRUE\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nTask 3: List Practice\n\n1) Create a new dataframe\n\n# create two new vectors of pre-measurements and post-measurements of the second experiment \npre_bp2 &lt;- c(138, 135, 147, 117, 152, 134, 114, 121, 131, 130)\npost_bp2 &lt;- c(105, 136, 123, 130, 134, 143, 135, 139, 120, 124)\n# create pre_bp2 and post_bp2 objects\nnames(pre_bp2) &lt;- paste(\"Subject\", 1:10, sep = \"_\")\nnames(post_bp2) &lt;- paste(\"Subject\", 1:10, sep = \"_\")\n\npre_bp2\n\n Subject_1  Subject_2  Subject_3  Subject_4  Subject_5  Subject_6  Subject_7 \n       138        135        147        117        152        134        114 \n Subject_8  Subject_9 Subject_10 \n       121        131        130 \n\npost_bp2\n\n Subject_1  Subject_2  Subject_3  Subject_4  Subject_5  Subject_6  Subject_7 \n       105        136        123        130        134        143        135 \n Subject_8  Subject_9 Subject_10 \n       139        120        124 \n\n# create diff_bp2 object\ndiff_bp2 &lt;- pre_bp2 - post_bp2\ndiff_bp2\n\n Subject_1  Subject_2  Subject_3  Subject_4  Subject_5  Subject_6  Subject_7 \n        33         -1         24        -13         18         -9        -21 \n Subject_8  Subject_9 Subject_10 \n       -18         11          6 \n\n# create post_less_120_2 object\npost_less_120_2 &lt;- post_bp2 &lt; 120\npost_less_120_2\n\n Subject_1  Subject_2  Subject_3  Subject_4  Subject_5  Subject_6  Subject_7 \n      TRUE      FALSE      FALSE      FALSE      FALSE      FALSE      FALSE \n Subject_8  Subject_9 Subject_10 \n     FALSE      FALSE      FALSE \n\n# create patients2 object\npatients2 &lt;- names(pre_bp2)\npatients2\n\n [1] \"Subject_1\"  \"Subject_2\"  \"Subject_3\"  \"Subject_4\"  \"Subject_5\" \n [6] \"Subject_6\"  \"Subject_7\"  \"Subject_8\"  \"Subject_9\"  \"Subject_10\"\n\n# create second data frame that is similar to the previous\nbp_df2 &lt;- data.frame(\n  patients = patients2,\n  pre_bp = pre_bp2,\n  post_bp = post_bp2,\n  diff_bp = diff_bp2,\n  post_under_120 = post_less_120_2\n)\nbp_df2\n\n             patients pre_bp post_bp diff_bp post_under_120\nSubject_1   Subject_1    138     105      33           TRUE\nSubject_2   Subject_2    135     136      -1          FALSE\nSubject_3   Subject_3    147     123      24          FALSE\nSubject_4   Subject_4    117     130     -13          FALSE\nSubject_5   Subject_5    152     134      18          FALSE\nSubject_6   Subject_6    134     143      -9          FALSE\nSubject_7   Subject_7    114     135     -21          FALSE\nSubject_8   Subject_8    121     139     -18          FALSE\nSubject_9   Subject_9    131     120      11          FALSE\nSubject_10 Subject_10    130     124       6          FALSE\n\n# print data frame nicely\nknitr::kable(bp_df2)\n\nWarning in attr(x, \"align\"): 'xfun::attr()' is deprecated.\nUse 'xfun::attr2()' instead.\nSee help(\"Deprecated\")\n\n\nWarning in attr(x, \"format\"): 'xfun::attr()' is deprecated.\nUse 'xfun::attr2()' instead.\nSee help(\"Deprecated\")\n\n\n\n\n\n\npatients\npre_bp\npost_bp\ndiff_bp\npost_under_120\n\n\n\n\nSubject_1\nSubject_1\n138\n105\n33\nTRUE\n\n\nSubject_2\nSubject_2\n135\n136\n-1\nFALSE\n\n\nSubject_3\nSubject_3\n147\n123\n24\nFALSE\n\n\nSubject_4\nSubject_4\n117\n130\n-13\nFALSE\n\n\nSubject_5\nSubject_5\n152\n134\n18\nFALSE\n\n\nSubject_6\nSubject_6\n134\n143\n-9\nFALSE\n\n\nSubject_7\nSubject_7\n114\n135\n-21\nFALSE\n\n\nSubject_8\nSubject_8\n121\n139\n-18\nFALSE\n\n\nSubject_9\nSubject_9\n131\n120\n11\nFALSE\n\n\nSubject_10\nSubject_10\n130\n124\n6\nFALSE\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n2) Create and store a list with two elements\n\n1st element named treatment and contains the first data frame\n2nd element named placebo and contains the second data frame\n\n\n# create treatment and placebo objects for each corresponding dataframe\ntreatment &lt;- bp_df1\nplacebo &lt;- bp_df2\n\n# create list with treatment and placebo using list()\nbp_exp &lt;- list(treatment = treatment, placebo = placebo)\nbp_exp\n\n$treatment\n              patient pre_bp post_bp diff_bp less_120\nSubject_1   Subject_1    130     114      16     TRUE\nSubject_2   Subject_2    128      98      30     TRUE\nSubject_3   Subject_3    116     113       3     TRUE\nSubject_4   Subject_4    124      99      25     TRUE\nSubject_5   Subject_5    133     107      26     TRUE\nSubject_6   Subject_6    134     116      18     TRUE\nSubject_7   Subject_7    118     113       5     TRUE\nSubject_8   Subject_8    126     111      15     TRUE\nSubject_9   Subject_9    114     119      -5     TRUE\nSubject_10 Subject_10    127     117      10     TRUE\nSubject_11 Subject_11    141     101      40     TRUE\nSubject_12 Subject_12    138     119      19     TRUE\nSubject_13 Subject_13    128     130      -2    FALSE\nSubject_14 Subject_14    140     122      18    FALSE\nSubject_15 Subject_15    137     106      31     TRUE\nSubject_16 Subject_16    131     106      25     TRUE\nSubject_17 Subject_17    120     124      -4    FALSE\nSubject_18 Subject_18    128     102      26     TRUE\nSubject_19 Subject_19    139     117      22     TRUE\nSubject_20 Subject_20    135     113      22     TRUE\n\n$placebo\n             patients pre_bp post_bp diff_bp post_under_120\nSubject_1   Subject_1    138     105      33           TRUE\nSubject_2   Subject_2    135     136      -1          FALSE\nSubject_3   Subject_3    147     123      24          FALSE\nSubject_4   Subject_4    117     130     -13          FALSE\nSubject_5   Subject_5    152     134      18          FALSE\nSubject_6   Subject_6    134     143      -9          FALSE\nSubject_7   Subject_7    114     135     -21          FALSE\nSubject_8   Subject_8    121     139     -18          FALSE\nSubject_9   Subject_9    131     120      11          FALSE\nSubject_10 Subject_10    130     124       6          FALSE\n\n\n\n\n3) Access the first list element using three different types of syntax.\n\n# first access using brackets [], returns a list containing the named element\nbp_exp[1]\n\n$treatment\n              patient pre_bp post_bp diff_bp less_120\nSubject_1   Subject_1    130     114      16     TRUE\nSubject_2   Subject_2    128      98      30     TRUE\nSubject_3   Subject_3    116     113       3     TRUE\nSubject_4   Subject_4    124      99      25     TRUE\nSubject_5   Subject_5    133     107      26     TRUE\nSubject_6   Subject_6    134     116      18     TRUE\nSubject_7   Subject_7    118     113       5     TRUE\nSubject_8   Subject_8    126     111      15     TRUE\nSubject_9   Subject_9    114     119      -5     TRUE\nSubject_10 Subject_10    127     117      10     TRUE\nSubject_11 Subject_11    141     101      40     TRUE\nSubject_12 Subject_12    138     119      19     TRUE\nSubject_13 Subject_13    128     130      -2    FALSE\nSubject_14 Subject_14    140     122      18    FALSE\nSubject_15 Subject_15    137     106      31     TRUE\nSubject_16 Subject_16    131     106      25     TRUE\nSubject_17 Subject_17    120     124      -4    FALSE\nSubject_18 Subject_18    128     102      26     TRUE\nSubject_19 Subject_19    139     117      22     TRUE\nSubject_20 Subject_20    135     113      22     TRUE\n\n# second access using double brackets [[]], returns the actual data frame \nbp_exp[[1]]\n\n              patient pre_bp post_bp diff_bp less_120\nSubject_1   Subject_1    130     114      16     TRUE\nSubject_2   Subject_2    128      98      30     TRUE\nSubject_3   Subject_3    116     113       3     TRUE\nSubject_4   Subject_4    124      99      25     TRUE\nSubject_5   Subject_5    133     107      26     TRUE\nSubject_6   Subject_6    134     116      18     TRUE\nSubject_7   Subject_7    118     113       5     TRUE\nSubject_8   Subject_8    126     111      15     TRUE\nSubject_9   Subject_9    114     119      -5     TRUE\nSubject_10 Subject_10    127     117      10     TRUE\nSubject_11 Subject_11    141     101      40     TRUE\nSubject_12 Subject_12    138     119      19     TRUE\nSubject_13 Subject_13    128     130      -2    FALSE\nSubject_14 Subject_14    140     122      18    FALSE\nSubject_15 Subject_15    137     106      31     TRUE\nSubject_16 Subject_16    131     106      25     TRUE\nSubject_17 Subject_17    120     124      -4    FALSE\nSubject_18 Subject_18    128     102      26     TRUE\nSubject_19 Subject_19    139     117      22     TRUE\nSubject_20 Subject_20    135     113      22     TRUE\n\n# third access using $, also returns just the data frame itself\nbp_exp$treatment\n\n              patient pre_bp post_bp diff_bp less_120\nSubject_1   Subject_1    130     114      16     TRUE\nSubject_2   Subject_2    128      98      30     TRUE\nSubject_3   Subject_3    116     113       3     TRUE\nSubject_4   Subject_4    124      99      25     TRUE\nSubject_5   Subject_5    133     107      26     TRUE\nSubject_6   Subject_6    134     116      18     TRUE\nSubject_7   Subject_7    118     113       5     TRUE\nSubject_8   Subject_8    126     111      15     TRUE\nSubject_9   Subject_9    114     119      -5     TRUE\nSubject_10 Subject_10    127     117      10     TRUE\nSubject_11 Subject_11    141     101      40     TRUE\nSubject_12 Subject_12    138     119      19     TRUE\nSubject_13 Subject_13    128     130      -2    FALSE\nSubject_14 Subject_14    140     122      18    FALSE\nSubject_15 Subject_15    137     106      31     TRUE\nSubject_16 Subject_16    131     106      25     TRUE\nSubject_17 Subject_17    120     124      -4    FALSE\nSubject_18 Subject_18    128     102      26     TRUE\nSubject_19 Subject_19    139     117      22     TRUE\nSubject_20 Subject_20    135     113      22     TRUE\n\n\n\n\n4) In one line, access the placebo data frame, pre_bp2 column.\n\n# use [] and [[]] to access the pre_bp2 column as a data frame\nbp_exp[[2]] [2:2]\n\n           pre_bp\nSubject_1     138\nSubject_2     135\nSubject_3     147\nSubject_4     117\nSubject_5     152\nSubject_6     134\nSubject_7     114\nSubject_8     121\nSubject_9     131\nSubject_10    130\n\n# or use $ and [] to do the same \nbp_exp$placebo[2]\n\n           pre_bp\nSubject_1     138\nSubject_2     135\nSubject_3     147\nSubject_4     117\nSubject_5     152\nSubject_6     134\nSubject_7     114\nSubject_8     121\nSubject_9     131\nSubject_10    130\n\n# or use just $ to return the vector only\nbp_exp$placebo$pre_bp\n\n [1] 138 135 147 117 152 134 114 121 131 130\n\n\n\n\n\nTask 4: Control Flow Practice\nCharacterize the post-treatment/placebo blood pressure as optimal (&lt;= 120), borderline (120 &lt; bp &lt;= 130), and high (&gt; 130).\n\n1) Create a new column in each data frame called status.\n\n# create a new \"status\" column in each data frame\nbp_exp$treatment$status &lt;- character(20)\nbp_exp$placebo$status &lt;- character(10)\n\n# check that the new columns are added\nbp_exp\n\n$treatment\n              patient pre_bp post_bp diff_bp less_120 status\nSubject_1   Subject_1    130     114      16     TRUE       \nSubject_2   Subject_2    128      98      30     TRUE       \nSubject_3   Subject_3    116     113       3     TRUE       \nSubject_4   Subject_4    124      99      25     TRUE       \nSubject_5   Subject_5    133     107      26     TRUE       \nSubject_6   Subject_6    134     116      18     TRUE       \nSubject_7   Subject_7    118     113       5     TRUE       \nSubject_8   Subject_8    126     111      15     TRUE       \nSubject_9   Subject_9    114     119      -5     TRUE       \nSubject_10 Subject_10    127     117      10     TRUE       \nSubject_11 Subject_11    141     101      40     TRUE       \nSubject_12 Subject_12    138     119      19     TRUE       \nSubject_13 Subject_13    128     130      -2    FALSE       \nSubject_14 Subject_14    140     122      18    FALSE       \nSubject_15 Subject_15    137     106      31     TRUE       \nSubject_16 Subject_16    131     106      25     TRUE       \nSubject_17 Subject_17    120     124      -4    FALSE       \nSubject_18 Subject_18    128     102      26     TRUE       \nSubject_19 Subject_19    139     117      22     TRUE       \nSubject_20 Subject_20    135     113      22     TRUE       \n\n$placebo\n             patients pre_bp post_bp diff_bp post_under_120 status\nSubject_1   Subject_1    138     105      33           TRUE       \nSubject_2   Subject_2    135     136      -1          FALSE       \nSubject_3   Subject_3    147     123      24          FALSE       \nSubject_4   Subject_4    117     130     -13          FALSE       \nSubject_5   Subject_5    152     134      18          FALSE       \nSubject_6   Subject_6    134     143      -9          FALSE       \nSubject_7   Subject_7    114     135     -21          FALSE       \nSubject_8   Subject_8    121     139     -18          FALSE       \nSubject_9   Subject_9    131     120      11          FALSE       \nSubject_10 Subject_10    130     124       6          FALSE       \n\n\n\n\n2) For the non-placebo data frame (within the list), create a for loop and use if/then/else logic to create the status column’s values.\n\n# use a for loop and if/then/else logic to create characterizations for the blood pressure measurements \n\nfor(i in 1:20){\n  if (bp_exp$treatment$post_bp[i] &lt;= 120) {\n    bp_exp$treatment$status[i] &lt;- \"optimal\"\n    \n  } else if (bp_exp$treatment$post_bp[i] &lt;= 130) {\n    bp_exp$treatment$status[i] &lt;- \"borderline\"\n    \n  } else {\n    bp_exp$treatment$status[i] &lt;- \"high\"\n  }\n}\n\n# check\nbp_exp$treatment\n\n              patient pre_bp post_bp diff_bp less_120     status\nSubject_1   Subject_1    130     114      16     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_2   Subject_2    128      98      30     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_3   Subject_3    116     113       3     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_4   Subject_4    124      99      25     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_5   Subject_5    133     107      26     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_6   Subject_6    134     116      18     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_7   Subject_7    118     113       5     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_8   Subject_8    126     111      15     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_9   Subject_9    114     119      -5     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_10 Subject_10    127     117      10     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_11 Subject_11    141     101      40     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_12 Subject_12    138     119      19     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_13 Subject_13    128     130      -2    FALSE borderline\nSubject_14 Subject_14    140     122      18    FALSE borderline\nSubject_15 Subject_15    137     106      31     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_16 Subject_16    131     106      25     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_17 Subject_17    120     124      -4    FALSE borderline\nSubject_18 Subject_18    128     102      26     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_19 Subject_19    139     117      22     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_20 Subject_20    135     113      22     TRUE    optimal\n\n\n\n\n3) Repeat for the placebo data frame (within the list).\n\n# repeat as before but with the placebo data frame\n\nfor(i in 1:10){\n  if (bp_exp$placebo$post_bp[i] &lt;= 120) {\n    bp_exp$placebo$status[i] &lt;- \"optimal\"\n    \n  } else if (bp_exp$placebo$post_bp[i] &lt;= 130) {\n    bp_exp$placebo$status[i] &lt;- \"borderline\"\n    \n  } else {\n    bp_exp$placebo$status[i] &lt;- \"high\"\n  }\n}\n\n# check\nbp_exp$placebo\n\n             patients pre_bp post_bp diff_bp post_under_120     status\nSubject_1   Subject_1    138     105      33           TRUE    optimal\nSubject_2   Subject_2    135     136      -1          FALSE       high\nSubject_3   Subject_3    147     123      24          FALSE borderline\nSubject_4   Subject_4    117     130     -13          FALSE borderline\nSubject_5   Subject_5    152     134      18          FALSE       high\nSubject_6   Subject_6    134     143      -9          FALSE       high\nSubject_7   Subject_7    114     135     -21          FALSE       high\nSubject_8   Subject_8    121     139     -18          FALSE       high\nSubject_9   Subject_9    131     120      11          FALSE    optimal\nSubject_10 Subject_10    130     124       6          FALSE borderline\n\n# check that they both show up on the list\nbp_exp\n\n$treatment\n              patient pre_bp post_bp diff_bp less_120     status\nSubject_1   Subject_1    130     114      16     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_2   Subject_2    128      98      30     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_3   Subject_3    116     113       3     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_4   Subject_4    124      99      25     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_5   Subject_5    133     107      26     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_6   Subject_6    134     116      18     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_7   Subject_7    118     113       5     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_8   Subject_8    126     111      15     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_9   Subject_9    114     119      -5     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_10 Subject_10    127     117      10     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_11 Subject_11    141     101      40     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_12 Subject_12    138     119      19     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_13 Subject_13    128     130      -2    FALSE borderline\nSubject_14 Subject_14    140     122      18    FALSE borderline\nSubject_15 Subject_15    137     106      31     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_16 Subject_16    131     106      25     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_17 Subject_17    120     124      -4    FALSE borderline\nSubject_18 Subject_18    128     102      26     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_19 Subject_19    139     117      22     TRUE    optimal\nSubject_20 Subject_20    135     113      22     TRUE    optimal\n\n$placebo\n             patients pre_bp post_bp diff_bp post_under_120     status\nSubject_1   Subject_1    138     105      33           TRUE    optimal\nSubject_2   Subject_2    135     136      -1          FALSE       high\nSubject_3   Subject_3    147     123      24          FALSE borderline\nSubject_4   Subject_4    117     130     -13          FALSE borderline\nSubject_5   Subject_5    152     134      18          FALSE       high\nSubject_6   Subject_6    134     143      -9          FALSE       high\nSubject_7   Subject_7    114     135     -21          FALSE       high\nSubject_8   Subject_8    121     139     -18          FALSE       high\nSubject_9   Subject_9    131     120      11          FALSE    optimal\nSubject_10 Subject_10    130     124       6          FALSE borderline"
